  "worked_solution": "We start by solving $3\\le 4x+7\\le 19:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & \\le 4x+7\\le 19 \\\\ -4 & \\le 4x\\le 12 \\\\ -1 & \\le x\\le 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14252895/assets/7d7303c5608dbd17911794959438f925d7ab6edd3ebba600d657b396ccf8eaa9.png)\n\nThen, we solve $6x+8<2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+8 & <2 \\\\ 6x & <-6 \\\\ x & <-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14252895/assets/ad5692ee92c2b67f50c0354020642bd9c711bdf7b88f4d476455512e33f9fc5f.png)\n\nTo graph the values of $x$ that satisfy either inequality, we overlay the two graphs on the same number line:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14252895/assets/638b44af1e33b78102a0dbfa3857c1292f200e026f54adf92fe77a97bf7e10a1.png)\n\nTherefore, the final solution is $x\\le 3.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
